This project includes 37 playbooks adapted from an upstream troubleshooting
baseline. The baseline commit and original attribution are preserved in the
project history; current files use generic host-Agent wording and
"author: upstream-maintainers". This project adds a portable Agent Skill
workflow, host-tool mappings, safety guidance, installers, validation, and
33 playbooks marked "source: local".

Upstream baseline repository:
https://github.com/noahapp/noah-for-tinkerers
Baseline commit: e5e5536bcb925358d65d8dc6812ee6c49722373b

Deviations from the upstream baseline
------------------------------------

The 37 "source: bundled" playbooks are adaptations, not verbatim copies. The
changes below were made on top of the baseline commit and are recorded here so
downstream users can tell adapted behavior from upstream behavior.

- Metadata. Every bundled playbook gained a "category" field used by this
  project's routing index and validator, and "last_reviewed" was refreshed to
  the review date. The "## Tools referenced" sections were rewritten to name
  the semantic tool aliases defined in "references/tool-contract.md" and the
  per-platform tool maps, replacing free-text tool descriptions.
- Cross-platform routing. Playbooks that shipped macOS-only commands under a
  cross-platform title now branch explicitly per platform, and references to
  other playbooks resolve to the platform-matched variant instead of always
  pointing at the macOS one. The largest such changes are in
  "playbook-health-baseline-check.md" and "playbook-local-data-audit.md",
  which gained Windows and Linux command paths and per-platform path maps.
- Windows Update ("playbook-windows-update-troubleshooting.md"). Removed
  "Get-WindowsUpdate" and "Hide-WindowsUpdate", which come from the
  third-party PSWindowsUpdate module and fail on a stock machine; replaced
  "net stop x && net start x" chains, which Windows PowerShell 5.1 does not
  support, with "Stop-Service"/"Start-Service"; added an availability probe
  before the MSDT troubleshooter, which Microsoft has been deprecating and
  removing since 2023, plus a Settings-based fallback; and corrected the
  "Get-HotFix" listing to select the columns it sorts on.
- OpenClaw uninstall ("playbook-setup-openclaw-uninstall.md"). A
  configuration-snapshot offer was inserted before any deletion and later
  steps were renumbered accordingly; wildcard recursive deletes were replaced
  by explicit literal paths shown to the user first; and the state directory
  is listed before removal.
- Safety wording. State-changing steps were aligned with
  "references/safety-policy.md": confirm before deleting, rename instead of
  delete where a rollback is possible, never disable security controls, and
  never echo secrets.
- Formatting only. Fenced blocks gained language tags and the files were
  normalized for markdownlint. These changes do not alter behavior.

Beyond the bundled playbooks, one project-level metadata change departs from the
upstream baseline. "skills/computer-repair-skill/SKILL.md" now declares the
optional "license: AGPL-3.0" frontmatter field, and the validator requires that
value to match the LICENSE file shipped alongside it. The upstream contributing
guide restricted the frontmatter to the required "name" and "description"
fields. "license" is part of the agent-skill frontmatter specification and is
ignored by clients that do not read it; it is declared here so a redistributed
copy of this skill states its license without depending on the surrounding
files.

